{\"response\": 90, \"explanation\": \"Using a binomial GLM (logit link) with missing teeth counts out of observable sockets as the response, and controlling for age, sex estimate, and tooth class, Homo sapiens was the reference genus. All non-human genera (Pan, Papio, Pongo) had significantly lower AMTL odds than Homo sapiens: Pan OR=0.28 (p≈3.4e-12), Papio OR=0.13 (p≈2.5e-9), Pongo OR=0.079 (p≈3.7e-4). Predicted missing-tooth probability at mean covariates was ~0.078 for Homo sapiens vs ~0.023 (Pan), ~0.011 (Papio), and ~0.0066 (Pongo). This consistent, statistically significant pattern indicates modern humans have higher AMTL frequencies after accounting for age, sex, and tooth class, warranting a strong “Yes”.\"}
